"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][22]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M22-L1 – Markov Decision Processes and Rewards<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13, M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate an MDP, calculate\nreturns and explain the role of the policy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>States, actions and feedback<\/h2>\n<p>An automated material buffer can trigger a delivery immediately or\nwait until the next shift. An early delivery costs money; a late one may\nhalt production. The action changes the future stock situation. A\nsupervised model that merely predicts consumption in the next hour\ntherefore does not yet decide when to order. Reinforcement learning (RL)\nseeks a decision rule with a good long-term outcome. The environment\nsupplies the consequences of decisions rather than a list of correct\nactions.<\/p>\n<p>A Markov decision process (MDP) consists of a state space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan action space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(possibly state-dependent), a transition and reward distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s&#39;,r\\mid s,a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a discount factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the discrete-time model, the agent observes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nchooses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreceives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{t+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand moves to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{t+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMarkov means that the distribution of the next state and reward, given\nthe state and action, does not additionally depend on the history. This\nholds only for a sufficient state representation. If information about\ndeliveries already ordered is missing, for instance, the model can be\ntreated as a partially observable process (POMDP) or the state can be\naugmented with relevant information. A history alone does not guarantee\nan exact Markov property.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Discounted return and policy<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001686_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Grouped bars compare discounted reward contributions and total returns of 4.1 and 0.9.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Grouped bars compare discounted reward\ncontributions and total returns of 4.1 and 0.9.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Discounted returns for two delivery policies.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The policy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(a\\mid s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the distribution of actions in state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder a deterministic policy, the selected action has probability one.\nAn episode ends at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nno further rewards arise thereafter. The return from step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_t=\\sum_{k=0}^{T-t-1}\\gamma^k R_{t+k+1},\\qquad G_t=R_{t+1}+\\gamma G_{t+1}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With an infinite horizon and bounded rewards,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq\\gamma&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuffices for absolute convergence. In finite episodes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis also possible. The factor is not a transition probability; it weights\nlater feedback. A finite time limit can make the optimal action depend\non the remaining time.<\/p>\n<p>As a synthetic comparison of two fully known sequences, consider\nimmediate delivery with rewards\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-4,0,10)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand waiting with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,-8,10)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rewards are already scaled in common utility points. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_0=-4+0+0.81\\cdot10=4.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>7.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8.1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0-7.2+8.1=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrespectively. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe results are −3.1 and −1.5 respectively. Which alternative is\npreferred depends on the objective. The diagram shows the discounted\ncontributions and sums for 0.9. In a stochastic environment, the\nexpected return rather than an individual return is optimised.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> gamma <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    g <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> r <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> gamma<span class=\"op\">**<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(g.<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(g, [<span class=\"fl\">4.1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> gamma<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> [<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">3.1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Value functions and model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The state value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\pi(s)=\\mathbb E_\\pi[G_t\\mid S_t=s]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand action value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_\\pi(s,a)=\\mathbb E_\\pi[G_t\\mid S_t=s,A_t=a]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiffer with respect to the first decision. Both assume that the\nspecified policy is followed after that decision. The optimisation\nobjective also depends on the initial state distribution. A new\noperating mode may change both the transitions and rewards; the\npreviously learned policy then cannot be transferred without\nevaluation.<\/p>\n<p>An MDP may be available as a known planning model or inferred from\nexperience. Simulated feedback is meaningful in practice only if the\nsimulation represents relevant mechanisms. The chosen reward is a proxy\nfor an objective and is not automatically identical to the real quality\nof a decision. Safety limits and partial observability are addressed\nseparately later.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>At which discount factor are both sequences equivalent? Solution: the\ncommon final contribution cancels;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4=-8\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor larger values, immediate delivery is preferable in this\ndeterministic comparison. A reward alone therefore does not describe the\nentire task.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262039246/reinforcement-learning/\">Sutton\nand Barto (2018): Reinforcement Learning, 2nd ed.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro.html\">OpenAI:\nSpinning Up, Part 1 – Key Concepts in RL<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · concepts, returns, value\nfunctions and Bellman equations<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidstarsilver.wordpress.com/teaching/\">David\nSilver: Reinforcement Learning, Advanced Topics 2015<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · course\noverview with lectures 2–9 opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M22-L2 – Bellman Equations and Dynamic Programming<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L1<\/a>;\nM13, M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive Bellman recursions and\ndistinguish value iteration from policy iteration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursion rather than exhaustive future search<\/h2>\n<p>If the transition model of a small MDP is known, future decisions can\nbe calculated without writing out every infinitely long action sequence.\nDecomposing the return into the immediate reward and discounted\nremainder yields the Bellman expectation equation<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\pi(s)=\\sum_a\\pi(a\\mid s)\\sum_{s&#39;,r}p(s&#39;,r\\mid s,a)[r+\\gamma v_\\pi(s&#39;)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In finite state and action spaces, a fixed policy can also be\nevaluated using a system of linear equations. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the transition matrix and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe vector of expected immediate rewards. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(I-\\gamma P_\\pi)v_\\pi=r_\\pi.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For an optimal policy, the Bellman optimality equation holds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_*(s)=\\max_a\\sum_{s&#39;,r}p(s&#39;,r\\mid s,a)[r+\\gamma v_*(s&#39;)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Maximisation takes place outside the expectation: the decision is\nmade before the random outcome of the action becomes visible. In finite\ndiscounted MDPs, a stationary deterministic optimal policy exists. Both\nthe fixed-policy mapping and the optimality operator are contractions in\nthe maximum norm with factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRepeated application therefore converges from any finite initial value\nto the respective unique fixed point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Teaching example: operating an inspection station<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001523_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two value-iteration trajectories approach fixed points near 11.67 and 8.33.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two value-iteration trajectories approach\nfixed points near 11.67 and 8.33.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Value iteration towards a Bellman fixed point.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A station is either ready (B) or exhausted (E); the original state\nlabels are retained. The permissible options in each state are work and\nrest. Transitions in this model world are deterministic:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th>Action<\/th>\n<th>Next state<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Reward<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>Work<\/td>\n<td>E<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>Rest<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E<\/td>\n<td>Work<\/td>\n<td>E<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E<\/td>\n<td>Rest<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the policy work in B, rest in E, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(B)=5+0.8v(E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(E)=-1+0.8v(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolving yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.36<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mover><mn>6<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(B)=4.2/0.36=11.\\overline 6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mover><mn>3<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(E)=8.\\overline 3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe alternative action in B gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mover><mn>3<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8v(B)=9.\\overline 3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin E it gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mover><mn>6<\/mn><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+0.8v(E)=7.\\overline 6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe policy under consideration is thus indeed optimal. The diagram shows\nhow both values evolve under synchronous value iteration.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>P <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>exact <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linalg.solve(np.eye(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>P,r)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>next_state <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>reward <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">150<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>reward<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v[next_state]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>q.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(v,exact)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.array_equal((reward<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v[next_state]).argmax(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(v.<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Policy iteration, value iteration and cost<\/h2>\n<p>Policy iteration alternates between complete (or sufficiently\naccurate) evaluation of a policy and its improvement by greedy\nselection. A fixed tie-breaking rule can prevent unnecessary switches.\nValue iteration combines the steps, performing an optimality backup each\ntime. With synchronous backups, the error bound for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|v_k-v_*\\|_\\infty\\leq\\gamma^k\\|v_0-v_*\\|_\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA verifiable Bellman residual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|Tv-v\\|_\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso provides a bound on the distance to the fixed point by dividing by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>In the dense finite case, directly representing all transitions may\nrequire up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(|\\mathcal S|^2|\\mathcal A|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstorage and work per value iteration step when expected rewards are\navailable. Sparse or factorised models can be considerably cheaper.\nLarge state spaces and unknown transitions motivate the sampling methods\nof the next lesson.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=(5,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the second step,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2(B)=\\max(5+0.8,4)=5.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2(E)=\\max(1.8,-1+4)=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA greedy step using an inaccurate value function may therefore select a\ndifferent policy from the one at the fixed point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262039246/reinforcement-learning/\">Sutton\nand Barto (2018): Reinforcement Learning, 2nd ed.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro.html\">OpenAI:\nSpinning Up, Part 1 – Key Concepts in RL<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · concepts, returns, value\nfunctions and Bellman equations<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidstarsilver.wordpress.com/teaching/\">David\nSilver: Reinforcement Learning, Advanced Topics 2015<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · course\noverview with lectures 2–9 opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M22-L3 – Monte Carlo and Temporal Difference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L2<\/a>;\nM13, M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate Monte Carlo, TD and\nmulti-step targets and explain bootstrapping.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning from episodes and transitions<\/h2>\n<p>When transition probabilities are unknown, an agent can estimate\nvalues from observed sequences. Monte Carlo evaluation for a fixed\npolicy uses complete episodes. In first-visit Monte Carlo, the return\nfollowing the first visit to a state in each episode is included in its\naverage. A sample mean estimate can be updated using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>←<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(s)\\leftarrow V(s)+\\alpha(G_t-V(s))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the running mean of the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-th\nsample,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=1/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIndependent restarts facilitate statistical arguments; repeated visits\nwithin an episode are not automatically independent.<\/p>\n<p>Temporal difference (TD) updates before an episode ends. The one-step\ntarget and TD error are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_t=R_{t+1}+\\gamma V(S_{t+1}),\\qquad \\delta_t=Y_t-V(S_t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For TD(0),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>←<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(S_t)\\leftarrow V(S_t)+\\alpha\\delta_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na terminal state has value zero. Bootstrapping means that a current\nestimate forms part of the target. TD can thus learn earlier and does\nnot require completed episodes. Early in learning, however, the target\ndepends on the potentially inaccurate value estimate. MC and TD share\nthe same desired fixed point under suitable conditions, but their\nsampling variability and transient biases differ.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing a particular episode<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001640_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Monte Carlo and TD(0) learning trajectories approach 6.5 from different starting estimates.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Monte Carlo and TD(0) learning\ntrajectories approach 6.5 from different starting\nestimates.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Monte Carlo and temporal-difference estimates.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A diagnostic procedure passes through A → B → End, returning first 2\nand then 6 points. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(A)=V(B)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand constant step size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe MC targets are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_A=6.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_B=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter one episode, the values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.25<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3.25,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTD(0) updates A immediately with the initially uninformative value of B:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(A)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nB is then set to 3. In the second identical episode, A learns from the\nalready improved value of B. The diagram illustrates this difference\nacross ten episodes.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mc<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> td<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    mc <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(np.array([<span class=\"fl\">6.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>mc)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    td[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>td[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>td[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    td[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>td[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(mc,[<span class=\"fl\">3.25<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(td,[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(mc,[<span class=\"fl\">6.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(td,[<span class=\"fl\">6.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(mc.<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),td.<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The difference in the first episode results from the timing of the\nupdate, not from generally inferior TD estimates. In stochastic tasks,\ndistant rewards can generate considerable variability. TD then uses an\nestimated remaining value, whereas MC includes all remaining randomness\nin its target.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multi-step methods and eligibility traces<\/h2>\n<p>An\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-step\nreturn combines observed rewards and bootstrapping:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_t^{(n)}=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\gamma^k R_{t+k+1}+\\gamma^n V(S_{t+n}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The sum ends earlier if a terminal state is reached first; the\nbootstrap term is then omitted. One-step TD and the complete episodic\nreturn are the two endpoints.\nTD(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ncombines different backup lengths; eligibility traces assign current TD\nerrors to recently visited states. For the simple tabular accumulating\ntrace initialised at zero,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_t(s)=\\gamma\\lambda e_{t-1}(s)+\\mathbf 1\\{S_t=s\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>←<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(s)\\leftarrow V(s)+\\alpha\\delta_t e_t(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith linear or nonlinear function approximation, variants and\nconvergence conditions must be considered separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What is the TD value of A after the second episode? Solution: the\nsecond target is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+0.75\\cdot3=4.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe update yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4.25<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+0.5(4.25-1)=2.625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nB increases to 4.5. By contrast, the MC value of A increases to\n4.875.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262039246/reinforcement-learning/\">Sutton\nand Barto (2018): Reinforcement Learning, 2nd ed.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidstarsilver.wordpress.com/teaching/\">David\nSilver: Reinforcement Learning, Advanced Topics 2015<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · course\noverview with lectures 2–9 opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M22-L4 – Q-Learning and Exploration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L3<\/a>;\nM13, M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain Q-learning and SARSA,\nplan exploration and implement a tabular agent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Control learning and action values<\/h2>\n<p>For control, evaluating a fixed policy is insufficient: the agent\nshould develop a better rule. A tabular Q-learning step computes<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>←<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(S_t,A_t)\\leftarrow Q(S_t,A_t)+\\alpha[R_{t+1}+\\gamma\\max_a Q(S_{t+1},a)-Q(S_t,A_t)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For terminal transitions, the bootstrap term is omitted. Q-learning\nis off-policy: the behaviour policy generating a transition may differ\nfrom the greedy target policy. In contrast, SARSA\n(State–Action–Reward–State–Action) uses in its target the next action\nvalue actually selected by its behaviour policy. This may matter when\nexploration errors occur near costly states. Under suitable tabular\nassumptions (a finite stationary MDP, sufficient visits to all\nstate–action pairs, appropriate stochastic approximation step sizes,\nbounded rewards and a discounted objective), Q-learning converges to the\noptimum. A constant learning rate does not generally provide this\nguarantee.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exploration in a fictional sorting facility<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001672_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Q-value estimates for immediate dispatch and an intermediate stop converge to 2 and 3.6.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Q-value estimates for immediate dispatch\nand an intermediate stop converge to 2 and 3.6.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Q-learning discovers a better route.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A parcel starts in S. Immediate dispatch ends with 2 points; an\nintermediate station Z is entered with 0 points. There, inspection ends\nthe episode with 4 points, while returning the parcel gives −2. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe two options in S have optimal values 2 and 3.6. Initially, however,\nall estimated values are zero. If an agent always selected the first\nmaximal entry and immediately received 2 points, it would never try the\nalternative route.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-greedy\nselects uniformly from all available actions with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\notherwise choosing a maximal Q entry. With a unique best action and two\noptions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives probability 0.9 for the best and 0.1 for the other. The following\nsmall learning run uses a constant rate and fixed seed; all transitions\nare deterministic. The diagram tracks both Q estimates in S.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">22<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros((<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> gamma<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> alpha<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> eps<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> episode <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">400<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(rng.integers(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> rng.random()<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>eps <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(q[s].argmax())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        done<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(s<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        target<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>r <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> done <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> r<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>gamma<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>].<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        q[s,a]<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span>alpha<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(target<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>q[s,a])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> done: <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(q,[[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.6<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]],atol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(q.<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exploration, data coverage and overestimation<\/h2>\n<p>A temperature in softmax action selection can adjust action\nprobabilities gradually; UCB methods augment bandit value estimates with\nuncertainty bonuses. A bandit considers immediate feedback and has no\naffected subsequent state. In an MDP, it is also crucial whether\nexploration adequately reaches distant states. Random actions may be\nimpermissible in real systems; only authorised, safeguarded experiments\nbelong in the learning process.<\/p>\n<p>The maximum operator can introduce optimism with noisy value\nestimates. For two unbiased but variable Q estimators, the expected\nmaximum is at least as large as the maximum of the expectations. Double\nQ-learning separates action selection from its evaluation using a second\nestimate; this reduces that mechanism, not every error. Off-policy\nlearning with function approximation and bootstrapping introduces\nadditional instability risks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">inspection<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(Z,\\text{inspection})=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">return<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(Z,\\text{return})=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">intermediate station<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(S,\\text{intermediate station})=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the next Q-learning value after entering Z. Solution: target\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=0+0.9\\cdot3=2.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnew value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.74<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=1.5+0.2(2.7-1.5)=1.74<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf returning the parcel were subsequently selected, SARSA would have\ntarget −0.9 and new value 1.02.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262039246/reinforcement-learning/\">Sutton\nand Barto (2018): Reinforcement Learning, 2nd ed.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro.html\">OpenAI:\nSpinning Up, Part 1 – Key Concepts in RL<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · concepts, returns, value\nfunctions and Bellman equations<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidstarsilver.wordpress.com/teaching/\">David\nSilver: Reinforcement Learning, Advanced Topics 2015<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · course\noverview with lectures 2–9 opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M22-L5 – Policy Gradients and Actor-Critic<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L4<\/a>;\nM13, M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a policy gradient and\nbaseline; understand actor-critic as the coupling of a policy and value\nestimation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Optimising a policy directly<\/h2>\n<p>Value-based methods derive decisions from value estimates.\nPolicy-gradient methods, by contrast, parametrise the action\ndistribution directly. This is particularly useful for stochastic\ndecisions and continuous action spaces. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe differentiable and the initial state distribution independent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe probability of a trajectory contains the policy factors and the\nunknown environmental dynamics. Since the latter do not depend directly\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntheir logarithmic derivative vanishes. For a finite episodic objective\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\theta)=\\mathbb E_\\theta[G_0]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe log-derivative trick yields, under regularity conditions,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla J(\\theta)=\\mathbb E_\\theta\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\\gamma^t\\,G_t\\,\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(A_t\\mid S_t)\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Earlier rewards can be removed from the objective at each time step\nbecause they precede the relevant action. The discounting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma^t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutside the return belongs to the defined initial-state objective here.\nREINFORCE estimates this expression from samples and performs gradient\nascent. Differentiable environmental transitions are not required, but\nthe policy derivative is. For actions with continuous density, their log\ndensity is used.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An analytical policy example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001667_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Logistic expected return rises from 2 to 5; its derivative is positive with a maximum of 0.75.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Logistic expected return rises from 2 to\n5; its derivative is positive with a maximum of 0.75.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Policy parameter and expected reward.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An inspection task permits a standard measurement (2 points) or a\nprecision measurement (5 points). In this one-step model, the\nprobability of precision measurement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\sigma(\\theta)=1/(1+\\exp(-\\theta))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expected value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\theta)=2+3p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J&#39;(\\theta)=3p(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nboth actions are equally likely and the derivative is 0.75. The score of\nthe Bernoulli policy for action\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a-p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA baseline can be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\mathbb E[R]=3.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor both actions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(R-b)(a-p)=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthen holds. Here the variance even falls to zero, which is not generally\nthe case. The diagram shows the expected value and derivative as\nfunctions of the policy parameter.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>sig<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x: <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>j<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x: <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sig(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>fd<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(j(h)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>j(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>sig(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>g<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>j(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(fd,<span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(g,<span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(fd,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>),g)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Actor, critic and advantage<\/h2>\n<p>A state-dependent, action-independent baseline may be subtracted from\nthe return without changing the expected score gradient:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_a\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)=\\nabla_\\theta 1=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_\\pi(s,a)=q_\\pi(s,a)-v_\\pi(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures the advantage over the policy’s average decision. Actor-critic\ncombines an actor (policy) and a critic (value estimation). A common\nteaching example uses the TD error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r+\\gamma V_\\phi(s&#39;)-V_\\phi(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas an advantage estimate. The critic is trained on a value target, and\nthe actor is updated in the direction of the log-policy gradient\nweighted by that estimate. During the actor step, the advantage estimate\nis treated as given. An inaccurate critic can introduce bias.<\/p>\n<p>A2C refers to advantage actor-critic updates collected synchronously.\nPPO uses a clipped probability ratio to limit incentives for large\npolicy changes; this is not a general guarantee of improvement or\nsafety. Off-policy actor-critic methods also differ in data usage and\ncorrections. An entropy bonus can encourage exploration, but with a\nfixed weight it changes the optimisation objective.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\log(4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=4.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the gradient. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.48<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot0.8\\cdot0.2=0.48<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlthough the better task is already selected frequently, the slope\nremains positive. Near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe logistic derivative becomes very small.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262039246/reinforcement-learning/\">Sutton\nand Barto (2018): Reinforcement Learning, 2nd ed.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro3.html\">OpenAI:\nSpinning Up, Part 3 – Intro to Policy Optimization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · policy-gradient\nderivation and baselines<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M22-L6 – Deep RL, Offline RL and Imitation Learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L5<\/a>;\nM13, M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate DQN targets and\ndistinguish offline distribution shift from behaviour imitation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Deep action-value models<\/h2>\n<p>With camera images, numerous sensors or continuous states, a table is\nusually impractical. Deep reinforcement learning uses neural networks to\napproximate a policy or value function. A deep Q-network (DQN) outputs\nestimated values for discrete actions. For a transition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s,a,r,s&#39;,d)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a genuine terminal state, a typical target is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/munder><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>B<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>B<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=r+\\gamma(1-d)\\max_{a&#39;}Q_{\\bar\\theta}(s&#39;,a&#39;),\\qquad L(\\theta)=\\frac1B\\sum_{i=1}^{B}(Q_\\theta(s_i,a_i)-y_i)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a temporarily fixed target network, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe batch size. The target is treated as constant during the gradient\nupdate. Experience replay stores transitions and draws batches from\nthem; this reduces immediate temporal correlation and permits reuse. The\nsamples are not thereby automatically independent or representative.\nTarget networks reduce rapid feedback between target and estimate. DQN\ndoes not handle continuous action spaces without adaptation;\nactor-critic methods may be more suitable. Time limits that truncate an\nepisode merely for data collection must be distinguished from genuine\nterminal states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Offline data and unobserved decisions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001659_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Observed action counts of 10 and 0 contrast with an unsupported value prediction and divergent counterfactual outcomes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Observed action counts of 10 and 0\ncontrast with an unsupported value prediction and divergent\ncounterfactual outcomes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Offline RL and missing data support.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In offline RL, the training dataset remains fixed. It was generated\nby an earlier policy; additional experiments during learning are\nexcluded. A greedy policy may switch to actions for which little or no\ndata exist. If the model overestimates their Q-values, bootstrapping may\namplify the error. Conservative methods or restrictions to adequately\nsupported actions aim to mitigate this. Support for the target policy in\nthe logged data is also important for evaluating it.<\/p>\n<p>In the simplified one-step case of an inspection device, there are\ntwo actions: standard S was observed ten times with 2 points; precision\nP was never performed. An extrapolating model estimates P at 7, but an\nunknown true utility could be −3 or +9 without changing these ten\nobservations. The diagram separates observed support from model\nestimates. The following code calculates the DQN target value for three\ninvented transitions; no network training takes place.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>count<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> est<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">7.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> est.argmax()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>supported<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.where(count<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,est,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.inf)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> supported.argmax()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> nxt<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>d)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>nxt.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> loss<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.mean((q<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(y,[<span class=\"fl\">4.6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(loss,<span class=\"fl\">3.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(y,<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(loss,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Imitation and learning from demonstrations<\/h2>\n<p>Behaviour cloning trains a policy as a supervised model from\nstate–action pairs in demonstrations, for example by minimising the\nnegative log-probability of the expert action. It requires no explicit\nnumerical reward, but also imitates undesirable habits and can\naccumulate errors in unseen states. DAgger (Dataset Aggregation)\ncollects states actually visited by the learning policy, queries an\nexpert there and extends the training dataset. This is an interactive\nmethod, not purely offline learning. It requires accessible expert\nfeedback and safeguarded data collection. Inverse reinforcement learning\ninstead seeks a plausible reward function for observed behaviour.\nSeveral reward functions can explain the same behaviour; without\nadditional assumptions, the result is not unique.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>In the DQN example, the third transition is marked as genuinely\nterminal. What target and overall loss result? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_3=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4.6<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.56<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.1867<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[(3-4.6)^2+0^2+(0+1)^2]/3=3.56/3\\approx1.1867<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIncorrect terminal marking therefore changes the learning objective.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25719670/\">Mnih et\nal. (2015): Human-level control through deep reinforcement learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01643\">Levine et al. (2020):\nOffline Reinforcement Learning: Tutorial, Review, and Perspectives on\nOpen Problems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · preprint opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v15/ross11a.html\">Ross,\nGordon and Bagnell (2011): A Reduction of Imitation Learning and\nStructured Prediction to No-Regret Online Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262039246/reinforcement-learning/\">Sutton\nand Barto (2018): Reinforcement Learning, 2nd ed.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M22-L7 – Safe Evaluation, Reward Design and Multi-Agent Learning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L6<\/a>;\nM13, M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish reward\nmisspecification, risk criteria, evaluation and multi-agent\nproblems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Performance is not equivalent to safety<\/h2>\n<p>A learning agent transporting materials receives one point for each\ncomponent delivered. If a sensor counts each repeated delivery of the\nsame component again, a policy can achieve high rewards without\nfulfilling the actual task. Exploitation of this proxy criterion is an\nexample of reward misspecification. A test must therefore measure actual\ngoal achievement, errors and safety violations separately from the\ntraining reward. Merely adjusting reward weights may be insufficient if\ncertain actions must never be permitted.<\/p>\n<p>A constrained MDP may have a cost variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{t+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin addition to the utility measure. One possible objective is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">subject to<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munder><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_\\pi\\mathbb E_\\pi[G_0]\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad\\mathbb E_\\pi\\left[\\sum_{t\\geq0}\\gamma^t C_{t+1}\\right]\\leq c_{\\max}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A Lagrange multiplier can incorporate this constraint into\noptimisation. An upper bound on expected costs, however, does not\nprotect every individual trajectory. Hard safety requirements\nnecessitate additional action restrictions, monitored emergency\nmechanisms and appropriate test design. The requirement must apply\nduring learning as well if real interactions occur then.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synthetic evaluation of a policy<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001689_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Policy A has mean reward 10 with no safety violations; policy B reaches 13 with three violations.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Policy A has mean reward 10 with no\nsafety violations; policy B reaches 13 with three\nviolations.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Reward versus safety violations.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Two transport rules A and B were each evaluated in ten comparable\nindependent test runs (using artificially generated metrics here). The\nutility scores are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[8,10,11,12,9,10,8,12,9,11]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor A and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[14,12,13,15,12,14,11,15,13,11]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor B. There are 0/10 and 3/10 safety violations respectively. The means\nare 10 and 13 respectively. Suppose a pre-defined acceptance rule in a\nteaching test requires no observed safety violation. B fails despite its\nhigher mean utility. A also receives no safety guarantee: with ten\nindependent Bernoulli trials and zero events, the exact one-sided 95%\nupper bound for event probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.05<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.259<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.05^{1/10}\\approx0.259<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith dependent runs, this interval is not necessarily valid. The diagram\npresents the two criteria side by side.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">13<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">13<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>upper<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> a.mean()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> b.mean()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">13<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.258<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>upper<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.260<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a.mean(),b.mean(),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(upper,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>In RL benchmarks, not only evaluation episodes but also training runs\nvary. Multiple seeds, separate test environments and confidence\nintervals for the actual unit of aggregation of interest are necessary.\nRepeated selection based on the test set introduces optimistic bias.\nImportance sampling is one approach for offline evaluation: for complete\nepisodes, the product\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\prod_t \\pi(A_t\\mid S_t)/\\mu(A_t\\mid S_t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a weight for converting from the behaviour policy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the target policy. This requires support and an appropriate dynamics\nand logging model. Long products can produce extreme variance; an\noffline estimate therefore does not replace a safety assessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multiple learning agents<\/h2>\n<p>If two transport robots operate simultaneously, each action also\ninfluences the other’s environment. In a cooperative Markov game, both\ncan receive a shared reward; with competing or mixed incentives, they\nhave individual rewards. If another agent is continuously updated, its\nbehaviour appears non-stationary from the perspective of an independent\nlearner. Centralised training with decentralised execution can use\nshared state information during training and replace it with locally\navailable information at execution time. Coordination, information\nlimits and credit assignment for a shared reward remain distinct\nproblems. Test partners should therefore be separated from training\npartners, and unfavourable combinations should also be investigated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>How many independent event-free test runs are needed at a minimum for\nthe exact one-sided 95% upper bound to be at most 5%? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.05<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.05^{1/n}\\leq0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.95<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>58.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq\\log(0.05)/\\log(0.95)\\approx58.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence 59. Even this result applies only to the defined test population\nand the assumed independent sampling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v16/garcia15a.html\">García and\nFernández (2015): A Comprehensive Survey on Safe Reinforcement\nLearning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/2021/hash/f514cec81cb148559cf475e7426eed5e-Abstract.html\">Agarwal\net al. (2021): Deep Reinforcement Learning at the Edge of the\nStatistical Precipice<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01643\">Levine et al. (2020):\nOffline Reinforcement Learning: Tutorial, Review, and Perspectives on\nOpen Problems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · preprint opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10462-022-10299-x\">Deep\nmultiagent reinforcement learning: challenges and directions (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· review opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M22-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M22-L8 – Game-Theoretic Models for Interacting Agents<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Reinforcement Learning and Sequential Decisions<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Advanced elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L7<\/a>;\nbasic linear algebra.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine Nash equilibria and\nminimax solutions in small games and explain the transition to Markov\ngames.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Strategic interaction<\/h2>\n<p>In an MDP, the best action depends on the environment and the agent’s\nown objective. Once other decision-making agents pursue their own\nobjectives, the best action may depend on their decisions. A finite\nnormal-form game contains players, their action sets and payoff\nfunctions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_i(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA pure strategy specifies an action; a mixed strategy is a distribution\nover actions. At a Nash equilibrium, no individual player can improve\ntheir expected payoff by switching unilaterally:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for all <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_i(\\sigma_i^*,\\sigma_{-i}^*)\\geq u_i(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_{-i}^*)\\quad\\text{for all }i,\\sigma_i.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here, for mixed strategies,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the expected payoff under independent randomisation. Equilibrium\nrepresents strategic stability, not necessarily high collective welfare.\nFinite normal-form games have at least one mixed Nash equilibrium, which\nmay also be pure. Multiple equilibria are possible. In the two-player\nzero-sum case, the players’ payoffs are opposites, making minimax\nmethods applicable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mixed equilibrium in a test game<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001657_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two payoff lines intersect at probability 0.5, the maximum of their lower envelope.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two payoff lines intersect at probability\n0.5, the maximum of their lower envelope.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Mixed strategies in a zero-sum game.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Two fictional agents play an abstract load-distribution task. The\nplanning agent A chooses channel X or Y; B chooses one of two test cases\nX or Y. The payoff to A is shown; B receives its negative:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">B: X<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">B: Y<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A: X<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A: Y<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>If A plays X with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits expected payoff against B:X is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3p-2(1-p)=5p-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand against B:Y is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-p+2(1-p)=2-3p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nB minimises, so A should maximise the smaller of the two results. The\nintersection occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5p-2=2-3p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a game value of 0.5. The diagram shows the two lines and their lower\nenvelope. If B plays X with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nA receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4q-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom X and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-4q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom Y. Equating them gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=3/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nagain with value 0.5. In none of the four pure strategy combinations are\nboth players simultaneously best-responding.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>M<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.375<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.625<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(p<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>M,[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(M<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>q,[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>grid<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linspace(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1001<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>low<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.minimum(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>grid<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>grid)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(grid[low.argmax()],<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>M<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>q)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Beyond zero-sum games<\/h2>\n<p>In general games, both players can receive positive or negative\npayoffs. Consider two providers each choosing data format A or B. If\nboth choose A, each receives 4; if both choose B, each receives 2; if\nthey choose different formats, both receive 0. Both diagonal entries are\npure Nash equilibria; the inferior stable outcome cannot be left by\nswitching unilaterally. A coordinated change could improve both players’\noutcomes. Pareto efficiency and Nash stability are therefore different\ncriteria. Correlated strategies allow shared random signals, provided\nrecommendations offer incentives to comply; they are not the same as an\nindependently mixed strategy profile.<\/p>\n<p>Markov games extend the static representation to states, joint\nactions and transitions. Each policy can account for future payoffs, so\nthe equilibrium depends on entire strategies rather than only current\nactions. Independent Q-learning does not guarantee a Nash equilibrium in\ngeneral Markov games. In zero-sum Markov games, however, under suitable\nfinite, discounted assumptions, a minimax backup can be considered for\neach state. Self-playing agents should not be evaluated exclusively\nagainst their own latest policy, since cyclic best responses and poor\ngeneralisation are possible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>In the test game, A always chooses X. What is B’s best response, and\nhow much improvement does A obtain from the mixed-strategy minimum\nguarantee? Solution: B:Y gives A −1, which is optimal from B’s\nperspective. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nA guarantees at least 0.5 in expectation, an improvement of 1.5 in the\nguaranteed payoff. An individual move can nevertheless yield −2.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.mit.edu/~6.7980/nfgs_nash.html\">MIT 6.7980:\nSetting and equilibria – the Nash equilibrium<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching page\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10462-022-10299-x\">Deep\nmultiagent reinforcement learning: challenges and directions (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· review opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
